  Why:  column "role" of relation "nextly_users" contains null values
  Next: Fix the cause the Why line names, then run the command again; \`--verbose\` shows where it failed.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/core-schema-apply-failed  (CORE_SCHEMA_APPLY_FAILED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > CORE_TABLES_NOT_CREATED 1`] = `
"✗ Core tables not found, and they could not be created.
  Why:  The schema push that creates them failed: permission denied for schema "public"
  Next: Run \`nextly migrate\` first to create the database schema.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/core-tables-not-created  (CORE_TABLES_NOT_CREATED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > CORE_TABLES_NOT_RECONCILED 1`] = `
"✗ Core tables could not be reconciled.
  Why:  Creating the system tables this version of Nextly adds failed on the existing database; \`--verbose\` shows the statement that failed.
  Next: Run \`nextly migrate\` to repair the schema before syncing.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/core-tables-not-reconciled  (CORE_TABLES_NOT_RECONCILED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > DATA_LOSS_CONSENT_MALFORMED 1`] = `
"✗ --accept-data-loss=dc_posts summary is not a list of tables and table.column pairs: "dc_posts summary" is not one.
  Why:  Each target is a table or a \`table.column\` pair, separated by commas. Nothing was changed.
  Next: Name tables and columns separated by commas (--accept-data-loss=posts,pages.summary), or give --accept-data-loss alone to consent to every deletion.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/data-loss-consent-malformed  (DATA_LOSS_CONSENT_MALFORMED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > DATA_LOSS_CONSENT_NOT_COVERED 1`] = `
"✗ The consent given does not cover 1 deletion.
  Why:  - column dc_posts.summary (42 non-empty values)
        Nothing was changed.
  Next: To delete this too: --accept-data-loss=dc_authors,dc_posts.summary (or NEXTLY_ACCEPT_DATA_LOSS=dc_authors,dc_posts.summary).
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/data-loss-consent-not-covered  (DATA_LOSS_CONSENT_NOT_COVERED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > DATA_LOSS_CONSENT_REQUIRED 1`] = `
"✗ Deleting data needs consent, and no terminal is attached to ask.
  Why:  This would delete:
        - column dc_posts.summary (42 non-empty values)
        Nothing was changed.
  Next: To consent: --accept-data-loss=dc_posts.summary (or NEXTLY_ACCEPT_DATA_LOSS=dc_posts.summary).
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/data-loss-consent-required  (DATA_LOSS_CONSENT_REQUIRED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > DATABASE_ADAPTER_MISSING 1`] = `
"✗ The MySQL adapter, \`@nextlyhq/adapter-mysql\`, is not installed.
  Why:  \`DATABASE_URL\` names a MySQL database, and Nextly reaches it through that package.
  Next: Install it in the project, for example \`pnpm add @nextlyhq/adapter-mysql\`, then run the command again.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/database-adapter-missing  (DATABASE_ADAPTER_MISSING)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > DATABASE_CATALOG_UNREADABLE 1`] = `
"✗ Could not read the database catalog, so core tables were not created.
  Why:  The catalog says which field-group registry this database uses, and creating the tables without it would leave a database that cannot repair itself.
  Next: Re-run once the database is reachable; \`--verbose\` shows why the catalog could not be read.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/database-catalog-unreadable  (DATABASE_CATALOG_UNREADABLE)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > DATABASE_DIALECT_INVALID 1`] = `
"✗ \`DB_DIALECT\` is \`mssql\`, which is not a database Nextly supports.
  Why:  Nextly runs on PostgreSQL, MySQL and SQLite.
  Next: Set \`DB_DIALECT\` to \`postgresql\`, \`mysql\` or \`sqlite\`, or remove it to read the type from \`DATABASE_URL\`.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/database-dialect-invalid  (DATABASE_DIALECT_INVALID)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > DATABASE_DIALECT_UNKNOWN 1`] = `
"✗ The database type could not be told from \`DATABASE_URL\`.
  Why:  It starts with none of \`postgres://\`, \`mysql://\` or \`file:\`, and \`DB_DIALECT\` is not set.
  Next: Set \`DB_DIALECT\` to \`postgresql\`, \`mysql\` or \`sqlite\` in \`.env\`.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/database-dialect-unknown  (DATABASE_DIALECT_UNKNOWN)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > DATABASE_NOT_BASELINED 1`] = `
"✗ Migration \`20261020_120000_000_initial\` cannot be applied: schema drift detected.
  Why:  This database already has tables, and this migration expects to start from an empty one. That is what a project managed by \`db:sync\` looks like the first time it creates a migration: the schema is real, but nothing has recorded where the history begins.
        Drift summary (1 differences):
          + table 'dc_posts' present in DB
  Next: Recovery — nothing in this history can be applied, so all of it has
        to go first. \`migrate:baseline\` refuses a project that has ANY
        migration or snapshot, and every file here was generated against a
        schema that already exists. Move the directory aside:
                mv '/home/you/app/migrations' '/home/you/app/migrations.pre-baseline'
        Or, if this is the only migration, remove just it and its snapshot
        (the glob covers per-dialect variants, which are one migration):
                rm '/home/you/app/migrations/20261020_120000_000_initial'*.sql '/home/you/app/migrations/meta/20261020_120000_000_initial.snapshot.json'
        Record what the database already has, once:
                pnpm nextly migrate:baseline
        Then re-create the migration; it will contain only what changed:
                pnpm nextly migrate:create --name initial
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/database-not-baselined  (DATABASE_NOT_BASELINED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > DATABASE_UNREACHABLE 1`] = `
"✗ The command could not connect to the PostgreSQL database.
  Why:  connect ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:5432
  Next: Check that the database is running and that \`DATABASE_URL\` names its host, port, database, user and password.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/database-unreachable  (DATABASE_UNREACHABLE)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > DATABASE_URL_MISSING 1`] = `
"✗ \`DATABASE_URL\` is not set, so there is no database to use.
  Why:  The command reads the database from \`DATABASE_URL\`, in the environment or the project's \`.env\` file.
  Next: Set \`DATABASE_URL\` in \`.env\`, for example \`DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@localhost:5432/app\`.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/database-url-missing  (DATABASE_URL_MISSING)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > DEMOTE_COLLECTION_NOT_FOUND 1`] = `
"✗ Collection 'posts' not found in nextly.config.ts collections.
  Why:  \`--demote\` moves a collection defined in \`nextly.config.ts\` to the Schema Builder, and its \`collections\` array has none with this slug.
  Next: Check the slug against the \`collections\` array in \`nextly.config.ts\`. A collection built in the Schema Builder is already there and needs no demotion.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/demote-collection-not-found  (DEMOTE_COLLECTION_NOT_FOUND)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > DROP_OF_FOREIGN_TABLE 1`] = `
"✗ A migration would drop a table that belongs to a different owner.
  Why:  In \`@acme/seo/20261020_120000_add_redirects\`, on \`dc_posts\`, which \`app\` holds. It has been refused, and nothing was applied.
  Next: Change only what this migration's owner holds: a change to another owner's table belongs in that owner's migrations.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/drop-of-foreign-table  (DROP_OF_FOREIGN_TABLE)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > FIELD_GROUP_BACKUP_NOT_CONFIRMED 1`] = `
"✗ \`nextly migrate:field-groups --apply\` did not run: it needs \`--backup-confirmed\`.
  Why:  Applying renames field-group tables and rewrites stored rows, so it runs only once you confirm a restorable backup of the database exists.
  Next: Take a backup of the database, then run \`nextly migrate:field-groups --apply --backup-confirmed\`.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/field-group-backup-not-confirmed  (FIELD_GROUP_BACKUP_NOT_CONFIRMED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > FIELD_GROUP_MIGRATION_IN_FLIGHT 1`] = `
"✗ The command did not run: a field-group storage migration is in flight on this database.
  Why:  Mid-run, some field-group tables carry their old names and some their new ones (it is at step 3), a shape neither the old configuration nor the new one describes.
  Next: Let the running migration finish, or, if it stopped, resume it with \`nextly migrate:field-groups --apply --backup-confirmed\`; then run this command again.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/field-group-migration-in-flight  (FIELD_GROUP_MIGRATION_IN_FLIGHT)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > FIELD_GROUP_STORAGE_REFUSED 1`] = `
"✗ The field-group storage migration refused to proceed.
  Why:  Registry rows name storage that does not exist.
  Next: Run the command again with \`--verbose\` to see the tables and rows involved, and check them before running anything that changes the schema.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/field-group-storage-refused  (FIELD_GROUP_STORAGE_REFUSED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > I18N_RESTORE_COLLECTION_REQUIRED 1`] = `
"✗ --collection <slug> is required.
  Why:  \`nextly i18n:restore\` brings back the archived translations of one entity, named by its slug.
  Next: Run \`nextly i18n:restore --collection <slug>\` with the slug of the collection, single or field group to restore.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/i18n-restore-collection-required  (I18N_RESTORE_COLLECTION_REQUIRED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > I18N_RESTORE_ENTITY_AMBIGUOUS 1`] = `
"✗ "hero" names a collection and a field group.
  Why:  Looked in your config and ui-schema.json. A field group's slugs are its own, so one slug can name a field group and a collection or single at once.
  Next: Say which with --kind collection or --kind field-group.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/i18n-restore-entity-ambiguous  (I18N_RESTORE_ENTITY_AMBIGUOUS)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > I18N_RESTORE_ENTITY_NOT_FOUND 1`] = `
"✗ No collection, single, or field group named "heroes" was found.
  Why:  Looked in your config and ui-schema.json.
  Next: Pass \`--collection\` the slug of a collection, single or field group your config or \`ui-schema.json\` defines. An entity removed since its translations were archived has to be added back first.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/i18n-restore-entity-not-found  (I18N_RESTORE_ENTITY_NOT_FOUND)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > I18N_RESTORE_KIND_INVALID 1`] = `
"✗ --kind must be collection, single or field-group, not "page".
  Why:  \`--kind\` says which kind of entity the slug names, when more than one kind has it.
  Next: Pass \`--kind collection\`, \`--kind single\` or \`--kind field-group\`, or leave \`--kind\` out when the slug names one entity.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/i18n-restore-kind-invalid  (I18N_RESTORE_KIND_INVALID)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > INTERRUPTED_BEFORE_START 1`] = `
"✗ \`nextly migrate\` was interrupted before it started.
  Why:  It was stopped while it waited for the schema lock, so it released its place and nothing was changed.
  Next: Run it again when you are ready.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/interrupted-before-start  (INTERRUPTED_BEFORE_START)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > LEGACY_BOOKKEEPING_FOUND 1`] = `
"✗ Legacy bookkeeping tables detected (nextly_migrations).
  Why:  They hold the history an earlier release recorded, which this release keeps in \`nextly_schema_events\`.
  Next: Run \`pnpm nextly upgrade\` once to consolidate them into nextly_schema_events before continuing.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/legacy-bookkeeping-found  (LEGACY_BOOKKEEPING_FOUND)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MEDIA_APPLY_OUTSIDE_PROJECT 1`] = `
"✗ Nothing was changed: --apply was run from outside /home/you/app.
  Why:  The database settings were read before --cwd named /home/you/app, so they may be another project's.
  Next: Run the command from /home/you/app to remove files.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/media-apply-outside-project  (MEDIA_APPLY_OUTSIDE_PROJECT)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MEDIA_CONFIG_OUTSIDE_PROJECT 1`] = `
"✗ Nothing was changed: the config ../other-project/nextly.config.ts is outside /home/you/app.
  Why:  /home/you/app's database settings would be read, so the config's storage may be another project's.
  Next: Run the command from that config's project to remove files.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/media-config-outside-project  (MEDIA_CONFIG_OUTSIDE_PROJECT)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MEDIA_STORAGE_UNLISTABLE 1`] = `
"✗ The media storage (uploadthing) cannot list its files, so it cannot be searched for left-behind thumbnails.
  Why:  The uploadthing storage adapter offers no listing of the files it holds. Nothing was changed.
  Next: Remove left-behind thumbnails with the storage provider's own tools, or use a storage plugin that can list its files.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/media-storage-unlistable  (MEDIA_STORAGE_UNLISTABLE)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MEDIA_THUMBNAILS_NOT_REMOVED 1`] = `
"✗ 1 left-behind thumbnail(s) were not removed.
  Why:  Could not remove media/2026/10/0b7f0b0e-thumb_x.png: AccessDenied: Access Denied
  Next: Run \`nextly media orphans --apply\` again to list and remove what is left.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/media-thumbnails-not-removed  (MEDIA_THUMBNAILS_NOT_REMOVED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MIGRATION_APPLY_FAILED 1`] = `
"✗ Migration \`20261020_120000_add_posts\` failed.
  Why:  relation "dc_authors" does not exist
  Next: Fix the cause the Why line names, then run the command again; \`--verbose\` shows where it failed.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/migration-apply-failed  (MIGRATION_APPLY_FAILED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MIGRATION_ATTEMPT_UNFINISHED 1`] = `
"✗ 20261020_120000_add_posts.sql runs outside a transaction, and its last attempt failed part-way.
  Why:  Running it again would repeat what of it ran and stayed: a statement that changes no schema may have run even where the schema shows nothing of the attempt, so it is not run again.
  Next: If you finish its remaining statements by hand, mark it applied with \`nextly migrate:resolve --applied 20261020_120000_add_posts.sql\` rather than running it again; if you reverse the statements that ran, run \`nextly migrate:resolve --failed-cleanup 20261020_120000_add_posts.sql\` and then \`nextly migrate\` again.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/migration-attempt-unfinished  (MIGRATION_ATTEMPT_UNFINISHED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MIGRATION_CONCURRENT_RUN 1`] = `
"✗ Another run recorded 20261020_120000_add_posts.sql applied while this one was running it.
  Why:  This run's statements were rolled back, and the ledger keeps the other run's record.
  Next: Run \`nextly migrate:status\` to see what the other run recorded; nothing of this run is left to undo.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/migration-concurrent-run  (MIGRATION_CONCURRENT_RUN)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MIGRATION_CREATE_CANCELLED 1`] = `
"✗ \`nextly migrate:create\` was cancelled, and wrote no migration.
  Why:  A prompt was closed before it was answered.
  Next: Run \`nextly migrate:create\` again when you are ready to answer its prompts.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/migration-create-cancelled  (MIGRATION_CREATE_CANCELLED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MIGRATION_DRIFT 1`] = `
"✗ Migration \`20261020_120000_add_posts\` cannot be applied: schema drift detected.
  Why:  Your database differs from BOTH the pre-migration baseline and the expected post-migration state. This usually means schema changes were made outside Nextly's tracked paths (manual SQL, a failed prior run, divergent teammate state).
        Drift summary (2 differences):
          + column 'dc_posts.subtitle' present in DB
          - column 'dc_posts.summary' absent from DB
  Next: Recovery (pick one):
          [A] Sync the DB to your config, then re-run migrate:
                pnpm nextly db:sync && pnpm nextly migrate
          [B] Mark it applied without executing (if you applied it manually):
                pnpm nextly migrate:resolve --applied 20261020_120000_add_posts
          [C] Capture the drift in a new migration:
                pnpm nextly migrate:create --name capture_drift && pnpm nextly migrate
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/migration-drift  (MIGRATION_DRIFT)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MIGRATION_FILE_EDITED 1`] = `
"✗ The migration file \`20261020_120000_add_posts.sql\` was edited after generation.
  Why:  Expected SHA-256: 9f2c4e1a7b3d5f60812a4c6e8b0d2f4a6c8e0b2d4f6a8c0e2b4d6f8a0c2e4b6d
        Actual SHA-256:   3b5d7f9a1c3e5b7d9f1a3c5e7b9d1f3a5c7e9b1d3f5a7c9e1b3d5f7a9c1e3b5d
  Next: Either revert the edit, or delete this file and re-generate via \`nextly migrate:create --name="<migration-name>"\`.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/migration-file-edited  (MIGRATION_FILE_EDITED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MIGRATION_FOREIGN_KEY_VIOLATION 1`] = `
"✗ This migration would leave 3 row(s) referencing rows that do not exist (dc_posts → dc_authors).
  Why:  It was rolled back, and nothing was applied.
  Next: Remove or repair the rows it leaves without a parent, in the migration or before it runs, then run it again.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/migration-foreign-key-violation  (MIGRATION_FOREIGN_KEY_VIOLATION)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MIGRATION_IRREVERSIBLE 1`] = `
"✗ 20261020_120000_add_posts.sql has no down SQL — it is irreversible.
  Why:  \`nextly migrate:down\` runs a migration's \`-- DOWN\` section, and this file's is empty.
  Next: Hand-write a -- DOWN section or use 'nextly migrate:fresh'.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/migration-irreversible  (MIGRATION_IRREVERSIBLE)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MIGRATION_MISSING 1`] = `
"✗ \`20261020_120000_add_posts.sql\` is recorded as applied, but its file is not in the migrations folder.
  Why:  It ran on this database and no removal of it is recorded, so the folder may be from an older build.
  Next: Restore the file from version control, or deploy the build that has it, then run \`nextly migrate\` again.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/migration-missing  (MIGRATION_MISSING)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MIGRATION_NAME_REQUIRED 1`] = `
"✗ \`nextly migrate:create\` needs a name for the migration.
  Why:  Only a \`--blank\` migration may be created without a name.
  Next: Name it, as in \`nextly migrate:create add_excerpt\` or \`--name=add_excerpt\`, or pass \`--blank\` for an empty migration.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/migration-name-required  (MIGRATION_NAME_REQUIRED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MIGRATION_PARTIALLY_APPLIED 1`] = `
"✗ 20261020_120000_add_posts.sql ran outside a transaction, and its statement 3 of 4 failed.
  Why:  column "summary" of relation "dc_posts" already exists
        The 2 statement(s) before it stayed applied, and were not undone.
  Next: If you finish its remaining statements by hand, mark it applied with \`nextly migrate:resolve --applied 20261020_120000_add_posts.sql\` rather than running it again; if you reverse the statements that ran, run \`nextly migrate:resolve --failed-cleanup 20261020_120000_add_posts.sql\` and then \`nextly migrate\` again.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/migration-partially-applied  (MIGRATION_PARTIALLY_APPLIED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MIGRATION_SNAPSHOT_INVALID 1`] = `
"✗ The snapshot file \`20261020_120000_add_posts.snapshot.json\` is invalid.
  Why:  JSON parse failed: Expected property name or '}' in JSON at position 1 (line 1 column 2)
  Next: Restore it from version control; if a newer version of Nextly wrote it, upgrade \`nextly\` to that version.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/migration-snapshot-invalid  (MIGRATION_SNAPSHOT_INVALID)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MIGRATION_SNAPSHOT_MISSING 1`] = `
"✗ \`20261020_120000_add_posts.sql\` has no paired snapshot file in /home/you/app/migrations/meta.
  Why:  Nothing is at \`20261020_120000_add_posts.snapshot.json\`, which \`migrate:create\` writes beside every migration; a migration generated before it wrote snapshots has none.
  Next: Either delete it, or re-create it with \`nextly migrate:create --blank\` and copy the SQL across.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/migration-snapshot-missing  (MIGRATION_SNAPSHOT_MISSING)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MIGRATION_SQL_UNCHECKABLE 1`] = `
