  Why:  A statement in \`@acme/seo/20261020_120000_add_redirects\` could not be read: unterminated block comment. It has been refused.
  Next: Rewrite that statement as plain SQL the guard can read; \`--verbose\` shows the statement.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/migration-sql-uncheckable  (MIGRATION_SQL_UNCHECKABLE)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > MIGRATION_TAMPERED 1`] = `
"✗ \`20261020_120000_add_posts.sql\` has changed since it was applied, so \`nextly migrate\` ran nothing.
  Why:  Its checksum no longer matches the one recorded when it ran on this database.
  Next: Restore the file as it was when it ran, and put the change in a new migration with \`nextly migrate:create\`.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/migration-tampered  (MIGRATION_TAMPERED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PACKAGE_INSTALL_FAILED 1`] = `
"✗ Install failed (pnpm exited 1).
  Why:  pnpm could not install \`@nextlyhq/plugin-seo\`; its own output above says why.
  Next: Fix what pnpm reports, then run \`nextly add @nextlyhq/plugin-seo\` again.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/package-install-failed  (PACKAGE_INSTALL_FAILED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PASSWORD_CLEAR_UNCONFIRMED 1`] = `
"✗ Nothing was changed: --apply also needs --confirm-default-hasher.
  Why:  --confirm-default-hasher is your statement that no stored password was written by a custom passwordHasher, now or earlier. A custom hasher's hashes can look like plain text here, and clearing them locks those people out.
  Next: If no custom passwordHasher ever wrote a password, run \`nextly users:clear-plaintext-passwords --apply --confirm-default-hasher\`.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/password-clear-unconfirmed  (PASSWORD_CLEAR_UNCONFIRMED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_ADMIN_SLUG_COLLISION 1`] = `
"✗ Plugins "@acme/reviews" and "acme-reviews" both resolve to the admin slug "acme-reviews", so they would share one admin address.
  Why:  A plugin's admin slug is made from its name, and these two names make the same one.
  Next: Rename one of them.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-admin-slug-collision  (PLUGIN_ADMIN_SLUG_COLLISION)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_ADMIN_WIDGET_NOT_JSON 1`] = `
"✗ Plugin "reviews" contributes an admin widget "stats" that is not JSON (keys: query).
  Why:  It is serialized into /api/admin-meta/workspace, so it may hold only strings, numbers, booleans, null, arrays and plain objects — no functions, class instances, Dates, Maps or bigints. A bigint in query.where throws while the whole workspace payload is serialized, which fails that request for every admin rather than just this card.
  Next: Remove \`query\` from the widget in \`contributes.admin.widgets\`, or give it a value of strings, numbers, booleans, null, arrays or plain objects.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-admin-widget-not-json  (PLUGIN_ADMIN_WIDGET_NOT_JSON)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_ADMIN_WIDGET_UNDRAWABLE 1`] = `
"✗ Plugin "reviews" contributes an admin widget "stats" that describes no body: it needs either a usable "component", or an "archetype" other than "custom" together with the "query" core draws it from.
  Why:  A widget is drawn one of two ways and must describe one of them. The type states this too, but it reaches a TypeScript caller and nothing else -- a plugin authored in JavaScript, or one whose manifest arrives as parsed JSON, reaches this check with nothing enforced.
  Next: Ship a \`component\` and draw the card yourself, or declare an \`archetype\` other than \`custom\` together with the \`query\` the host draws it from. The cell is keyed on \`id\`, so that must carry real text whichever way you choose.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-admin-widget-undrawable  (PLUGIN_ADMIN_WIDGET_UNDRAWABLE)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_APPLIED_MIGRATION_CHANGED 1`] = `
"✗ Plugin "@acme/seo" migration 20261020_120000_add_redirects: a plugin migration that has already been applied no longer matches what was applied.
  Why:  The database does not match the file that claims to describe it.
  Next: Restore the migration as it was when it ran.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-applied-migration-changed  (PLUGIN_APPLIED_MIGRATION_CHANGED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_APPLIED_MIGRATION_REMOVED 1`] = `
"✗ Plugin "@acme/seo" no longer lists its migration "20261020_120000_add_redirects", which the database records as applied.
  Why:  A plugin's applied migrations are never removed or renamed: the database holds what they did, and the migrations after them were written for that. Nothing was applied.
  Next: Restore "20261020_120000_add_redirects" under its name. To take it out, roll it back first with \`nextly migrate:down --plugin @acme/seo\` while it is still listed.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-applied-migration-removed  (PLUGIN_APPLIED_MIGRATION_REMOVED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_AUDIT_KIND_DUPLICATE 1`] = `
"✗ Plugin "reviews" declares the audit kind "reviews.approved" more than once.
  Why:  An audit kind is declared once, with every metadata key it records.
  Next: Declare it once with the full list of metadata keys.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-audit-kind-duplicate  (PLUGIN_AUDIT_KIND_DUPLICATE)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_AUDIT_KIND_INVALID 1`] = `
"✗ Plugin "reviews" declares the audit kind "reviews.Approved!".
  Why:  After "reviews." a kind may use only lowercase letters, digits, ".", "_" and "-", starting with a letter or digit.
  Next: Rename the audit kind using only those characters.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-audit-kind-invalid  (PLUGIN_AUDIT_KIND_INVALID)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_AUDIT_KIND_OUTSIDE_NAMESPACE 1`] = `
"✗ Plugin "reviews" declares the audit kind "review.approved", which must start with "reviews.".
  Why:  A plugin's audit kinds are named under its admin slug, so no two plugins record the same kind.
  Next: Rename the audit kind "review.approved" so that it starts with "reviews.".
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-audit-kind-outside-namespace  (PLUGIN_AUDIT_KIND_OUTSIDE_NAMESPACE)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_AUDIT_KIND_TOO_LONG 1`] = `
"✗ Plugin "reviews" declares the audit kind "reviews.approved-by-a-moderator-after-a-second-report-was-filed-late", which is longer than the 64 characters the audit trail's kind column holds.
  Why:  The audit trail stores each entry's kind in a column of that width.
  Next: Shorten the audit kind to 64 characters or fewer.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-audit-kind-too-long  (PLUGIN_AUDIT_KIND_TOO_LONG)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_CAPABILITY_ADDED_BY_SETUP 1`] = `
"✗ A setup transformer added capabilities.db.rawSql to plugin "reports", whose own manifest does not declare it.
  Why:  That capability grants the live database handle at ctx.db.raw, which reaches every table, and the app's listing covers only a plugin that declares it in its own manifest under the listed name.
  Next: Declare capabilities.db.rawSql in the manifest of the plugin the app configures, rather than in a setup transformer.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-capability-added-by-setup  (PLUGIN_CAPABILITY_ADDED_BY_SETUP)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_CAPABILITY_MISSING 1`] = `
"✗ Plugin "reports" requires the capability "email", which no enabled plugin provides.
