exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_MIGRATION_ADDS_UNFILLABLE_COLUMN 1`] = `
"✗ This migration would add seo_redirects.status as NOT NULL with no default to a table plugin "@acme/seo" created earlier, and the rows already in it would have no value for it.
  Why:  SQLite refuses such a column, PostgreSQL refuses it once the table has rows, and MySQL fills the rows with 0 or '' without saying so, so it is refused on every dialect when the migration is generated.
  Next: Give the column a default, or write the addition by hand — add the column nullable, fill the existing rows, then make it NOT NULL — in a module from \`nextly migrate:create --plugin <entry> --blank\`, which records this change for the SQL you write.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-migration-adds-unfillable-column  (PLUGIN_MIGRATION_ADDS_UNFILLABLE_COLUMN)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_MIGRATION_BEFORE_APPLIED 1`] = `
"✗ Plugin "@acme/seo" lists a migration "20261019_090000_add_index" that has not run, ordered before "20261020_120000_add_redirects", which has.
  Why:  A new migration may only follow the ones already applied: run now, it would run after migrations written to come after it. Nothing was applied.
  Next: Rename it so its name sorts after "20261020_120000_add_redirects", as the generator's timestamps do. If it was rolled back by hand, roll back the migrations after it first, or once its changes are in place record it with \`nextly migrate:resolve --applied plugin:@acme/seo/20261019_090000_add_index\`.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-migration-before-applied  (PLUGIN_MIGRATION_BEFORE_APPLIED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_MIGRATION_CHANGED 1`] = `
"✗ Plugin "@acme/seo" migration 20261020_120000_add_redirects: a plugin migration has been changed since it was generated.
  Why:  An edited module is refused rather than re-hashed, so the SQL that runs is the SQL that was reviewed. Nothing was applied.
  Next: Regenerate it, or restore the original. A module written or edited by hand is sealed with \`migrationChecksum\` from \`@nextlyhq/plugin-sdk/schema\` — \`checksum: migrationChecksum(content)\` over the module without its checksum — which is the form \`nextly migrate:create --plugin <entry> --blank\` and \`--no-transaction\` write.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-migration-changed  (PLUGIN_MIGRATION_CHANGED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_MIGRATION_DROPS_CREATED_SCHEMA 1`] = `
"✗ This migration would drop seo_redirects.target, which an earlier migration of plugin "@acme/seo" created, and lose its rows.
  Why:  The generator cannot tell a rename from a drop and an add, so it does not drop what the plugin created on its own.
  Next: If it was renamed, write the rename by hand; if the drop is deliberate, write the drop by hand — in a module from \`nextly migrate:create --plugin <entry> --blank\`, which records this change for the SQL you write.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-migration-drops-created-schema  (PLUGIN_MIGRATION_DROPS_CREATED_SCHEMA)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_MIGRATION_NOT_SHIPPED 1`] = `
"✗ "@acme/seo" has applied migrations its configured version no longer ships (20261020_120000_add_redirects), so they cannot be undone.
  Why:  A migration's DOWN lives in the version of the plugin that shipped it. Nothing was changed.
  Next: Configure the version that shipped them and uninstall again, or use --keep-data to leave its tables in place.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-migration-not-shipped  (PLUGIN_MIGRATION_NOT_SHIPPED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_MIGRATION_SQL_MISSING 1`] = `
"✗ Plugin "@acme/seo" migration 20261020_120000_add_redirects changes the schema on sqlite but has no sqlite statements, so running it would record a change it never made.
  Why:  A module written for its author's SQL, such as one from \`nextly migrate:create --plugin <entry> --blank\`, runs only the statements it is given. Nothing was applied.
  Next: Write the module's sqlite \`up\` and \`down\` in the plugin, then run \`nextly migrate\` again.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-migration-sql-missing  (PLUGIN_MIGRATION_SQL_MISSING)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_MIGRATIONS_OUT_OF_ORDER 1`] = `
"✗ Plugin "@acme/seo" lists its migration "20261019_090000_add_index" after "20261020_120000_add_redirects", but its migrations run in the order of their names, so "20261019_090000_add_index" runs first.
  Why:  Every reader of a plugin's migrations runs them by name, so a list in another order is refused rather than sorted in silence. Nothing was applied.
  Next: List the plugin's migrations in the order of their names (the order \`nextly migrate:create --plugin\` writes them in).
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-migrations-out-of-order  (PLUGIN_MIGRATIONS_OUT_OF_ORDER)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_NAME_TOO_LONG_FOR_SETTINGS 1`] = `
