  Next: Check the options this plugin is given in \`nextly.config.ts\`, or update the plugin; \`--verbose\` shows where it failed.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-setup-failed  (PLUGIN_SETUP_FAILED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_SHIPS_NO_MIGRATIONS 1`] = `
"✗ A plugin declares tables or table elements but ships no migrations to create them.
  Why:  They exist in development through push, and a deployed database gets its tables only from migrations. Not migrated: seo_redirects, dc_posts.seo_title.
  Next: Generate the plugin's migrations before deploying, with \`nextly migrate:create --plugin <entry>\`.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-ships-no-migrations  (PLUGIN_SHIPS_NO_MIGRATIONS)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_SLUG_COLLISION 1`] = `
"✗ Duplicate collection slug "reviews" contributed by code and reviews.
  Why:  A slug names one collection, single or field group, and "reviews" is claimed twice; "code" is \`nextly.config.ts\`.
  Next: Rename the entity in \`nextly.config.ts\`, or rename the plugin's with \`.rename()\`, for example \`plugin.rename({ "reviews": "new-slug" })\`.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-slug-collision  (PLUGIN_SLUG_COLLISION)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_TABLE_IDENTITY_UNREADABLE 1`] = `
"✗ The database reports no identity for seo_redirects, so whether @acme/seo's onUninstall leaves it in place could not be checked.
  Why:  A full uninstall reads each table's identity before and after the plugin's \`onUninstall\`, so its DOWN migrations never drop a table the hook put in place of the one they were judged against. Nothing was changed.
  Next: Use --keep-data to leave its tables in place.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-table-identity-unreadable  (PLUGIN_TABLE_IDENTITY_UNREADABLE)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_TABLES_NOT_MIGRATED 1`] = `
"✗ @acme/seo owns 1 table(s), listed above, but this database has no applied migration module to undo them.
  Why:  They were created by a development push rather than by a migration. Its tables: seo_redirects. Nothing was changed.
  Next: Drop them with a migration, or remove the plugin from config and reset the development database.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-tables-not-migrated  (PLUGIN_TABLES_NOT_MIGRATED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_UNINSTALL_DECLINED 1`] = `
"✗ Nothing was changed: the deletion was not confirmed.
  Why:  A full uninstall drops the plugin's tables, and the prompt that listed them was not answered yes.
  Next: Run the uninstall again and confirm the deletion, or use --keep-data to leave the tables in place.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-uninstall-declined  (PLUGIN_UNINSTALL_DECLINED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_UNINSTALL_IRREVERSIBLE 1`] = `
"✗ "@acme/seo" has migrations that cannot be undone (20261020_120000_add_redirects).
  Why:  A full uninstall undoes every migration or none: stopping at the first that cannot be undone would leave the plugin's schema half undone. Nothing was changed.
  Next: Use --keep-data to leave its tables in place.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-uninstall-irreversible  (PLUGIN_UNINSTALL_IRREVERSIBLE)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_UNINSTALL_KEEP_DATA_MISMATCH 1`] = `
"✗ The uninstall of @acme/seo began without --keep-data and did not finish.
  Why:  Run again, an uninstall carries on from where it stopped, and only the way it began: a full one may have dropped tables already, and one that kept its data has run \`onUninstall\` for tables it leaves. Nothing was changed.
  Next: Run it again without --keep-data to finish it.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-uninstall-keep-data-mismatch  (PLUGIN_UNINSTALL_KEEP_DATA_MISMATCH)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_UNINSTALL_UNFINISHED 1`] = `
"✗ The uninstall of the plugin "@acme/seo" began and did not finish.
  Why:  A plugin removed part way is refused in every environment, so its code never runs over the tables that are left.
  Next: Run "nextly plugins uninstall @acme/seo" to finish it, or "nextly plugins install @acme/seo" to reinstate it.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-uninstall-unfinished  (PLUGIN_UNINSTALL_UNFINISHED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PLUGIN_UNINSTALLED_BUT_CONFIGURED 1`] = `
"✗ The plugin "@acme/seo" has been uninstalled but is still listed in your config.
  Why:  Started again, it would run over tables its uninstall removed, and a development push would create them again, so it is refused in every environment.
  Next: Run "nextly plugins install @acme/seo" to reinstate it, or remove it from the config.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/plugin-uninstalled-but-configured  (PLUGIN_UNINSTALLED_BUT_CONFIGURED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > POSTGRES_SCHEMA_INVALID 1`] = `
"✗ db.postgres.schema is "Tenant-A", which is not a usable schema name.
  Why:  The name reaches SQL through the connection's \`search_path\`, where an unusable one would connect and then find nothing.
  Next: Use lower-case letters, digits and underscores, starting with a letter or underscore (max 63 characters), and not beginning with "pg_".
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/postgres-schema-invalid  (POSTGRES_SCHEMA_INVALID)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > POSTGRES_SCHEMA_MISMATCH 1`] = `
"✗ db.postgres.schema is not set (so "public"), but the database adapter passed to Nextly uses schema "tenant_a".
  Why:  Both must name the same schema, or tables and migrations end up split between them.
  Next: To fix it, create the adapter without a schema option, or set db.postgres.schema to "tenant_a".
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/postgres-schema-mismatch  (POSTGRES_SCHEMA_MISMATCH)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > POSTGRES_SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED 1`] = `
"✗ db.postgres.schema is "tenant_a", but a PostgreSQL schema other than "public" is not supported yet.
  Why:  Nextly can only create and migrate its tables in "public".
  Next: Remove db.postgres.schema (and any schema option on the database adapter), or set it to "public".
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/postgres-schema-unsupported  (POSTGRES_SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PRODUCTION_ROLLBACK_UNCONFIRMED 1`] = `
"✗ Refusing to roll back in production without --yes.
  Why:  \`NODE_ENV\` is \`production\`. Prefer rolling forward with a corrective migration, or restore from backup.
  Next: Re-run with --yes to override.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/production-rollback-unconfirmed  (PRODUCTION_ROLLBACK_UNCONFIRMED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PROJECT_ALREADY_INITIALIZED 1`] = `
"✗ Nextly is already initialized in this directory.
  Why:  /home/you/app/nextly.config.ts already exists.
  Next: Use \`--force\` to overwrite the existing configuration.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/project-already-initialized  (PROJECT_ALREADY_INITIALIZED)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > PROMOTE_COLLECTION_NOT_FOUND 1`] = `
"✗ Collection 'posts' not found in UI source (dynamic_collections).
  Why:  \`--promote\` moves a collection built in the Schema Builder into code, and no collection with this slug was built there.
  Next: Check the slug in the admin's Schema Builder. A collection already defined in \`nextly.config.ts\` is in code and needs no promotion.
  Docs: https://nextlyhq.com/docs/cli/errors/promote-collection-not-found  (PROMOTE_COLLECTION_NOT_FOUND)"
`;

exports[`each code, as a command ends with it > RELATION_TARGET_UNKNOWN 1`] = `
